    <div class="topic-title-lg">The Market Is Too Sure the Fed Hikes in December</div>
    <p class="topic-intro">
      Futures price roughly a 90% chance that the Fed raises rates again on 9 December. I think a pause is far more likely than 1-in-10: jobs growth has stalled, housing is weakening, and much of the inflation jump is an energy shock the Fed can't fix with higher rates. I express that view with a small, capped-risk bet that pays if the Fed holds.
    </p>

    <div class="worked">
      <div class="worked-title">Trade ticket</div>
      <div class="dt-wrap"><table class="dt" style="margin:0">
        <tr><td>Instrument</td><td>Options on December 2026 Three-Month SOFR futures (SR3Z6), expiring 11 Dec 2026</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Structure</td><td>Buy 95.875 call, sell 96.00 call (a 12.5 basis point bull call spread)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Entry</td><td>Pay no more than <strong>3.0 bp</strong> ($75) per spread</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Size</td><td>10 spreads &rarr; maximum loss <strong>$750</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maximum gain</td><td>9.5 bp per spread &rarr; <strong>$2,375</strong> on 10 spreads (about 3.2&times; the risk)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wins if</td><td>The Fed holds on 9 December</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Break-even odds</td><td>A hold needs at least a <strong>24%</strong> chance; the market says ~10%, I say ~30%</td></tr>
      </table></div>
    </div>

    <div class="metric-row">
      <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">Market odds of a hold</div><div class="metric-val">~10%</div><div class="metric-sub">CME FedWatch, 22 Oct</div></div>
      <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">My odds of a hold</div><div class="metric-val">~30%</div><div class="metric-sub">see "My View"</div></div>
      <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">Risk : reward</div><div class="metric-val">1 : 3.2</div><div class="metric-sub">at a 3 bp entry</div></div>
      <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">Expected value</div><div class="metric-val">+$188</div><div class="metric-sub">10 spreads, if I'm right on odds</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight">
      <div class="insight-lbl">The idea in one line</div>
      I'm not betting that a hold is <strong>likely</strong>. I'm betting it's <strong>more likely than the price says</strong>. When something is priced at 10% but you think it's 30%, a cheap bet on it can be worth making, even though you'll usually lose it.
    </div>

    <div class="topic-nav">
      <button class="tn-btn primary" style="margin-left:auto" onclick="switchTopic(2)">Next: What the Market Thinks &rarr;</button>
    </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ════════════ 2: MARKET ════════════ -->
    <div class="topic-pane" id="topic-2" style="display:none">
    <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="badge badge-accent">Section 1</span><span class="badge badge-subtle">The starting point</span></div>
    <div class="topic-title-lg">What the Market Thinks</div>
    <p class="topic-intro">Before having a view, you need to know what's already in the price. Right now, the market is close to certain the Fed hikes in December.</p>

    <div class="sh">The see-saw: price = 100 &minus; rate</div>
    <p class="np">A SOFR future is quoted as <strong>100 minus the interest rate people expect</strong> over a three-month window. If traders expect 4.18%, the future trades at 95.82. Rate and price move like two ends of a see-saw: <strong>if expected rates fall, the price rises</strong>. One basis point (0.01%) is worth $25 per contract.</p>
    <div class="f-box"><span class="math">Futures price = 100 &minus; expected average SOFR</span></div>

    <div class="sh">Snapshot &mdash; 22&ndash;23 October 2026</div>
    <div class="dt-wrap"><table class="dt">
      <tr><th>Measure</th><th>Level</th><th>What it tells us</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Fed target range</td><td>3.75&ndash;4.00%</td><td>After a unanimous hike on 16 September, the first in three years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SOFR (overnight)</td><td>3.88%</td><td>Where overnight borrowing actually sits (21 Oct)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SR3Z6 future</td><td>&asymp; 95.82</td><td>Market expects SOFR to average &asymp;4.18% from mid-Dec to mid-Mar</td></tr>
      <tr><td>October hike odds</td><td>&asymp; 16%</td><td>A pause next week is expected</td></tr>
      <tr class="hl"><td>December hike odds</td><td>&asymp; 90%</td><td>A December hike is treated as almost certain</td></tr>
    </table></div>

    <div class="insight">
      <div class="insight-lbl">Reading the price</div>
      Overnight SOFR is 3.88% but the December future implies 4.18%. That extra &asymp;30 bp is the market saying: <strong>one more 25 bp hike in December, plus a little chance of more early next year</strong>. If the Fed holds instead, that expected rate has to fall, and the future has to rise.
    </div>

    <div class="topic-nav">
      <button class="tn-btn" onclick="switchTopic(1)">&larr; Summary</button>
      <button class="tn-btn primary" style="margin-left:auto" onclick="switchTopic(3)">Next: My View &rarr;</button>
    </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ════════════ 3: VIEW ════════════ -->
    <div class="topic-pane" id="topic-3" style="display:none">
    <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="badge badge-accent">Section 2</span><span class="badge badge-subtle">Why I disagree</span></div>
    <div class="topic-title-lg">Why a Pause Is Underpriced</div>
    <p class="topic-intro">My case rests on data, not on guessing what any politician wants. Here's the evidence for a hold, and the evidence against me, side by side.</p>

    <div class="summary-grid">
      <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon green">1</div><div class="sum-title">Jobs growth has stalled</div><div class="sum-body">September payrolls rose by just <strong>29,000</strong>, far below forecasts, and unemployment rose to <strong>4.2%</strong>. Hiking into a weakening jobs market risks tipping it over.</div></div>
      <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon green">2</div><div class="sum-title">The inflation jump is mostly energy</div><div class="sum-body">Headline CPI hit <strong>3.7%</strong>, but gasoline (+34% year on year) did much of the work. Core CPI is <strong>2.6%</strong>. Higher rates can't produce more oil, and central banks often look through supply shocks like this.</div></div>
      <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon green">3</div><div class="sum-title">Housing is already cracking</div><div class="sum-body">September housing starts fell and pending home sales dropped to a <strong>record low</strong>. The rate-sensitive parts of the economy are already slowing.</div></div>
      <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon green">4</div><div class="sum-title">The Fed hasn't committed</div><div class="sum-body">Chair Kevin Warsh declined to say when, or whether, further hikes would come, and senior officials have sounded more cautious since September. A 90% price leaves no room for that doubt.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="sh">The case against me</div>
    <div class="dt-wrap"><table class="dt">
      <tr><th>Argument for a hike</th><th>My response</th></tr>
      <tr><td>September minutes said another hike would likely be appropriate by year-end</td><td>That was before the weak jobs and housing data. The minutes also said decisions depend on incoming data.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Headline inflation is rising, not falling (3.4% &rarr; 3.7%)</td><td>True, and it's the biggest risk to this trade. But it's energy-led, and core rose only modestly.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Other central banks are hiking (the ECB raised rates)</td><td>Each bank sets policy for its own economy; US jobs data is weaker.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The economy has otherwise been strong</td><td>Yes, which is why I put a hold at ~30%, not 50%+. I'm arguing it's underpriced, not that it's the most likely outcome.</td></tr>
    </table></div>

    <div class="insight">
      <div class="insight-lbl">Background, not a reason</div>
      The US midterm elections on 3 November add political noise around rates. I don't rely on it: the Fed is independent, and the December meeting comes a month after the vote. If anything, the Fed will want any decision to look purely data-driven.
    </div>

    <div class="topic-nav">
      <button class="tn-btn" onclick="switchTopic(2)">&larr; What the Market Thinks</button>
      <button class="tn-btn primary" style="margin-left:auto" onclick="switchTopic(4)">Next: The Trade &rarr;</button>
    </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ════════════ 4: TRADE ════════════ -->
    <div class="topic-pane" id="topic-4" style="display:none">
    <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="badge badge-accent">Section 3</span><span class="badge badge-subtle">Structure &middot; strikes &middot; payoff</span></div>
    <div class="topic-title-lg">Turning the View Into a Trade</div>
    <p class="topic-intro">I want something that pays well if the Fed holds, costs little if I'm wrong, and can't lose more than I put in. A bull call spread on the December SOFR future does all three.</p>

    <div class="sh">Why SR3Z6, and why these dates line up</div>
    <p class="np">SR3Z6's options expire on <strong>11 December</strong>, just two days after the Fed's decision on 9 December, so the result is fully in the price before my options settle. The future itself prices the <strong>average rate from 16 December to mid-March</strong>, a window that includes the next (late-January) meeting. So a hold only helps fully if the market doesn't immediately price a January hike instead.</p>

    <div class="sh">Why these strikes</div>
    <p class="np">I worked out where the future should trade on 11 December under five outcomes, by averaging the expected overnight rate across the 91-day window. The 95.875 / 96.00 strikes sit neatly between the "hike" and "hold" clusters.</p>
    <div class="dt-wrap"><table class="dt">
      <tr><th>Outcome on 9 Dec</th><th>Expected avg rate</th><th>SR3Z6 price</th><th>Spread pays</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Hike, more hikes signalled</td><td>4.21%</td><td>95.79</td><td>0 bp</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hike, "last one" tone</td><td>4.13%</td><td>95.87</td><td>0 bp</td></tr>
      <tr class="hl"><td>Hold, January hike fully priced</td><td>4.01%</td><td>95.99</td><td>11.3 bp</td></tr>
      <tr class="hl"><td>Hold, hikes on pause</td><td>3.92%</td><td>96.08</td><td>12.5 bp (max)</td></tr>
      <tr class="hl"><td>Surprise cut</td><td>3.63%</td><td>96.37</td><td>12.5 bp (max)</td></tr>
    </table></div>
    <div class="insight">
      <div class="insight-lbl">The design choice</div>
      Even in the least helpful hold case &mdash; the Fed pauses but everyone expects a January hike &mdash; the spread still pays <strong>11.3 of a possible 12.5 bp</strong>. That's deliberate: the trade should reward being right about December without needing me to also be right about January.
    </div>

    <div class="chart-block">
      <div class="chart-block-title">Profit or Loss at Expiry &mdash; 10 Spreads, 3 bp Entry</div>
      <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chartPay"></canvas></div>
      <div class="chart-controls">
        <div class="chart-control-row"><span class="cc-label">SR3Z6 on 11 Dec</span><input type="range" id="p-f" min="95.70" max="96.25" step="0.005" value="95.82"><span class="cc-value" id="p-f-v">95.820</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="live-out" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr)">
        <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">Implied rate</div><div class="metric-val" id="p-r">&ndash;</div></div>
        <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">Spread pays</div><div class="metric-val" id="p-bp">&ndash;</div></div>
        <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">Profit / loss</div><div class="metric-val" id="p-usd">&ndash;</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="sh">Is it worth it? Expected value</div>
    <p class="np">I'll lose this bet most of the time, so the question isn't "will it win?" but "<strong>does it pay enough when it wins?</strong>" Move the sliders: the trade only makes sense if your odds of a hold are above the break-even line.</p>
    <div class="chart-block">
      <div class="chart-block-title">Expected Value per 10 Spreads</div>
      <div class="chart-wrap" style="height:240px"><canvas id="chartEV"></canvas></div>
      <div class="chart-controls">
        <div class="chart-control-row"><span class="cc-label">My hold odds</span><input type="range" id="e-p" min="5" max="60" step="1" value="30"><span class="cc-value" id="e-p-v">30%</span></div>
        <div class="chart-control-row"><span class="cc-label">Entry cost</span><input type="range" id="e-c" min="1" max="6" step="0.25" value="3"><span class="cc-value" id="e-c-v">3.00 bp</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="live-out" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr)">
        <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">Break-even odds</div><div class="metric-val" id="e-be">&ndash;</div></div>
        <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">EV at my odds</div><div class="metric-val" id="e-me">&ndash;</div></div>
        <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">EV at market odds (10%)</div><div class="metric-val" id="e-mk">&ndash;</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="worked">
      <div class="worked-title">The arithmetic</div>
      <div class="worked-body">
        <div class="worked-math">Max gain   = 12.5 &minus; 3.0 = 9.5 bp      Max loss = 3.0 bp
Break-even = cost &divide; width = 3.0 &divide; 12.5 = 24%
EV (30%)   = 0.30 &times; 9.5 &minus; 0.70 &times; 3.0 = +0.75 bp  = +$18.75 per spread
EV (10%)   = 0.10 &times; 9.5 &minus; 0.90 &times; 3.0 = &minus;1.75 bp  = &minus;$43.75 per spread</div>
        If the market's 10% is right, this is a losing trade. Its value comes entirely from my disagreement with the market &mdash; which is why the next section matters.
      </div>
    </div>

