    <!-- ════════════ 5: RISKS ════════════ -->
    <div class="topic-pane" id="topic-5" style="display:none">
    <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="badge badge-accent">Section 4</span><span class="badge badge-subtle">What could go wrong</span></div>
    <div class="topic-title-lg">Risks, Catalysts and the Plan</div>
    <p class="topic-intro">A trade is only as good as the plan for managing it. Here's what I'm watching, what would prove me wrong, and what I'll do.</p>

    <div class="sh">Dates that will move this trade</div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="flow-step"><div class="fs-n">27&ndash;28 Oct</div><div class="fs-t">October FOMC</div><div class="fs-b">Pause expected. The statement and press conference tone set up December.</div></div>
      <div class="flow-step"><div class="fs-n">29 Oct</div><div class="fs-t">September PCE</div><div class="fs-b">The Fed's preferred inflation gauge.</div></div>
      <div class="flow-step"><div class="fs-n">November</div><div class="fs-t">Jobs &amp; CPI</div><div class="fs-b">October payrolls and inflation &mdash; the biggest swing factors.</div></div>
      <div class="flow-step"><div class="fs-n">9 &rarr; 11 Dec</div><div class="fs-t">Decision &amp; expiry</div><div class="fs-b">Fed decides; options settle two days later.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="sh">Main risks</div>
    <div class="dt-wrap"><table class="dt">
      <tr><th>Risk</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Energy shock spreads</td><td>If higher oil starts lifting core inflation, the Fed hikes and the spread expires worthless.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Jobs rebound</td><td>A strong October payrolls number would undo my strongest argument.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hold, but hawkish</td><td>A pause paired with a clear "January hike" signal reduces the payout (to ~11 bp in my model, more if the market prices extra hikes).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Execution cost</td><td>Options have bid-ask spreads. If the spread can't be bought at 3 bp or less, the maths no longer works and I don't trade.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Year-end funding</td><td>SOFR often jumps briefly around 31 December; small for a 91-day average, but it nudges the expected rate up.</td></tr>
    </table></div>

    <div class="sh">Rules</div>
    <div class="summary-grid">
      <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon blue">&rarr;</div><div class="sum-title">Entry</div><div class="sum-body">Buy 10 spreads only at <strong>3.0 bp or less</strong>. No chasing a higher price.</div></div>
      <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon green">&uarr;</div><div class="sum-title">Take profit</div><div class="sum-body">If dovish data lifts the spread to <strong>&ge; 9 bp</strong> before the meeting, sell half and keep the rest for the decision.</div></div>
      <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon amber">!</div><div class="sum-title">What changes my mind</div><div class="sum-body">October payrolls above ~150k, <em>or</em> core CPI clearly accelerating, <em>or</em> Fed officials openly endorsing a December hike. Then I close and accept the loss.</div></div>
      <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon purple">&#9633;</div><div class="sum-title">Never</div><div class="sum-body">Add to the position if it moves against me. The $750 maximum loss is the whole risk budget.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="qbox">
      <div class="qbox-lbl">After 11 December</div>
      <p class="qbox-body">I'll publish a follow-up breaking down what happened: how much of the result came from the Fed decision, from changes in expectations for January, and from time decay. Whatever the result, the point is to compare my reasoning with what actually happened.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="topic-nav">
      <button class="tn-btn" onclick="switchTopic(4)">&larr; The Trade</button>
