    <!-- ════════════ 6: METHOD ════════════ -->
    <div class="topic-pane" id="topic-6" style="display:none">
    <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="badge badge-accent">Section 5</span><span class="badge badge-subtle">How I got the numbers</span></div>
    <div class="topic-title-lg">Method, Limits and Sources</div>

    <div class="sh">Scenario prices</div>
    <p class="np">The future settles on the average SOFR over 16 Dec 2026 &ndash; 17 Mar 2027 (91 days). For each outcome, I take SOFR's current position in the Fed's range (3.88%), shift it by the December decision, and add any January hike in proportion to how much of the window comes after a late-January meeting (about 48 of 91 days):</p>
    <div class="code">def sr3z6_price(sofr_after_dec, p_jan_hike):
    avg_rate = sofr_after_dec + 0.25 * p_jan_hike * 48 / 91
    return 100 - avg_rate

# e.g. hold, January hike fully priced:
sr3z6_price(3.88, 1.0)   # -> 95.988  -> spread pays 11.3 bp</div>

    <div class="sh">Why I set an entry limit instead of a model price</div>
    <p class="np">A standard bell-curve options model (Bachelier, with an assumed 50&ndash;90 bp yearly volatility) values this spread at <strong>3.3&ndash;4.5 bp</strong>. Weighting my scenario payoffs by the market's own 90/10 odds gives about <strong>1.3 bp</strong>. They disagree because a Fed meeting doesn't produce one smooth range of outcomes: it produces two clusters, "hike" and "hold". So I don't trust either number. I set a limit of 3 bp, where the trade still works at my odds, and check a live quote before trading.</p>

    <div class="insight">
      <div class="insight-lbl">Limits of this memo</div>
      Prices are snapshots from 22&ndash;23 October 2026 and will move. The late-January meeting date is an assumption. Option premiums are estimates, not live quotes. This is a paper trade written for learning.
    </div>

    <div class="sh">Sources</div>
    <ul class="np" style="padding-left:18px">
